      "id": 1,
      "text": "Leakesville, Mississippi, takes its name from Walter Leake, the state’s third governor, who served in the early 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leakesville%2C_Mississippi",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1903 the Vinegar Bend Lumber Company extended a railroad to Leakesville, linking the town to Pascagoula.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.leakesvillems.com/about-2",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Greene County Museum, housed on the fourth floor of the courthouse at 400 Main Street, showcases artifacts from Native American times to today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/leakesville_mississippi",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Leakesville’s Main Street Organization runs a Preservation Committee that encourages volunteers to maintain the town’s historic downtown architecture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/leakesville_mississippi",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Every year, locals reenact the 1864 Battle of McLeod’s Mill, a Civil War skirmish near Leakesville, to honor history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/leakesville_mississippi",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Leakesville sits on the west side of the Chickasawhay River, a south‑flowing tributary of the Pascagoula.",
